# Git

A question is not permission. `reset`, `rebase`, `push --force`, `push --force-with-lease`, `branch -D`, `clean`, `checkout -- <path>`, and `worktree remove --force` run only on an order that names the operation and the target SHA or ref. That holds for the agent's own uncommitted edit too: undo it with an edit. Before removing any worktree, run `git -C <worktree> status --short`; undo what it lists, then remove it without `--force`, from outside the worktree.

A PR is merged with a merge commit, so `main` holds every commit of the branch, and `git branch -d <branch>` can delete the local branch. `-d` checks the branch against its upstream ref while that ref exists (`origin/<branch>` after `git push -u`), and against `HEAD` once the ref is gone; GitHub deletes the merged branch, and `git fetch --prune` then drops the ref. Measured in a scratch repository on 2026-11-28: with the ref, `-d` deleted a branch that `HEAD` did not hold, with the warning "merged to 'refs/remotes/origin/<branch>', but not yet merged to HEAD"; after the prune, with a `main` not brought forward, it refused with "not fully merged". So the check that counts is against `origin/main`, and the steps are:

1. Remove the branch's worktree (above).
2. `git fetch origin`, without `--prune`.
3. `git merge-base --is-ancestor <branch> origin/main`. Exit 0 means `main` holds the branch's head.
4. `git branch -d <branch>`. It changes no working tree.

When step 3 does not exit 0 (a commit made after the merged head, or one never pushed), or `-d` refuses after the ref is gone, keep the branch and ask the owner, with the branch name and its SHA. Do not bring the main working tree's `main` forward for it: the owner may have that tree on another branch (below). `-D` is not the next step. After PR #381, a squash merge, the local branch `fix/327-audit-cut-off` (23242f0) was deleted with `-D` without an order. Its commit stayed reachable at `refs/pull/381/head`. `gh pr merge --delete-branch` is not used either: it deletes the local branch too, and how it deletes it was not checked.

A conflict that another PR's merge caused is solved by merging `origin/main` into the branch (`git merge origin/main`), without asking: nothing is rewritten, and the reviewed commits stay. A rebase and a force-push still need an order. Until 2026-11-13 either way was asked first; with rows in parallel every second merge conflicts in the roadmap and the design tables (the owner's decision, Issue #372, after PR #367).

Such a conflict keeps both sides. Where two PRs added at the same place (the end of design §20.7, the end of `docs/design-decisions.md`, the list of done rows in `docs/roadmap.md`, the table of accepted threats in design §5.4 and the table of dependencies in design §21, whose rows have no ID), `main`'s part comes first and the branch's part after it. Since Issue #407 a row's record is a file of its own in `docs/rows/`, and the list of done rows is gone, so two rows' PRs meet only where they change the same section: after PRs #387 to #394 each merge made every other open PR conflict at the three ends. In PR #520 the merge 2e41a42 conflicted in the §5.4 table, where both sides had added a row after the same row; it kept both rows, but put the branch's row before `main`'s PostgreSQL row of V1d26b. Table rows are matched by their ID, or in §21 by their package: a row that `main` has takes `main`'s form, and a row that the branch added stays as it is. In PR #519 the merge 20205e3 conflicted where the branch had added the `Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.WindowsServices` row just above the `Npgsql` row that `main` had changed (V1d26b); the `Npgsql` row took `main`'s form, and the branch's row stayed where it was, above it. Where both sides added to one row or one method, both additions stay. Git may pair unrelated lines of tests that both sides appended, such as doc comments and closing braces: keep every test of both sides, and compare the counts of `[Fact]` and `[Theory]` before and after. After PR #377 merged, `origin/main` was merged into the branches of PRs #381, #382, #384, #387, #388, #390, and #391 this way: both PRs' additions to one table row of `docs/guide/reference/logs.md` were kept, both checks in `src/ElasticLM.Licensing/Limits.cs` (PR #382), and every test of two test files in PR #391.

After the lower PR of a stack merges (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`, Stacked PRs), the merge of `origin/main` into the upper PR can conflict in the files of the lower PR. When `main`'s copy of those files is the lower PR's last head (`git diff --quiet <lower head> origin/main -- <files>` exits 0) and that head is an ancestor of the upper branch (`git merge-base --is-ancestor <lower head> HEAD`), the upper branch's side of those files is kept: it already holds the lower PR's version and builds on it. The file is written back to that side as an edit, from the text of `git show HEAD:<file>`; `git checkout --ours -- <file>` is a `checkout -- <path>` and needs an order (above). The other conflicts are solved as above. In PR #526 the merge cb79c15 of 4758d74 conflicted in `docs/design.ja.md` and three test files; `main`'s copies of the test files were PR #505's last head 117da92, an ancestor of PR #526, so the branch's side was kept, and the design took `main`'s file with the three lines PR #526 had changed. PR #505 was a squash merge (4758d74 has one parent). With a merge commit, the lower PR's head is in `main`, and whether such a conflict still comes has not been measured.

After a conflict in code, every test of the projects the merge touched runs on the merged tree before the push, as for `Limits.cs` in PR #382. The merged tree is built even when git reports no conflict. In PR #390 a new test of the branch did not set the required member `LeaseSpec.AnsweredProofId`, which V1d5 had added on `main`; git reported no conflict, and the build failed with CS9035. After a failed build, `dotnet test --no-build` runs the earlier binaries, so its result is not used.

After the merge, and before the push, read the records of the rows the merge brought in (`docs/rows/<ID>.md`, their rows in `docs/roadmap.md`), above all what they say about properties the code now has and findings outside their row. A property of another row can break a test of the branch that the merge left untouched. In PR #418 the merge brought in V1d6's 30-second limit on a wait of the fake clock (PR #400), which `docs/rows/V1d6.md` already stated, and a test of the branch that moved the clock in small steps failed on it.

When asked about such an operation, answer with the current `HEAD` SHA, the target SHA, what would disappear, and the exact command. Then wait.

- Finished work goes to a PR in the same turn, without waiting for an ask: commit on the Issue's branch, push it, and open the PR (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`). The owner reviews on the PR; uncommitted changes on this machine cannot be reviewed. Do not stop at "may I commit?".
- Long work runs in an agent worktree (`git worktree add .dev/<branch> <branch>`), not in the main working tree: the owner may switch that tree to another branch to review.
- This covers the Issue's own branch only. Never commit to `main` or push to it, and never merge. Turning on auto-merge for a PR (`gh pr merge <n> --auto`) is a merge too, and needs the same order that names the PR (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`, step 7).
- A merge that the owner orders (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`, step 7) makes a merge commit, names the head it merges, and carries its own message: `gh pr merge <n> --merge --match-head-commit <sha> --subject "<PR title> (#<n>)" --body <text>`, where the text is a summary, a line `Refs #<issue>`, and the `Co-Authored-By` line. The merges of PR #530 (ef95b07) and PR #534 (ecb00c8) are merge commits made in this form. Without `--subject` and `--body`, GitHub writes the repository's default message, "Merge pull request #<n> from <owner>/<branch>" with the PR title as its body, as in the owner's merges of 2026-11-28 (PRs #536, #537, #512, #532, #538, #541, and #539). The message's body holds no closing verb: the `Closes` of the PR body closes the Issue (`.agents/skills/github-closing/SKILL.md`). The SHA for `--match-head-commit` is copied from the output of `git rev-parse HEAD`. On 2026-11-13 the owner ordered the squash merges of PRs #381, #382, and #384. The squash body of PR #381 held `Closes #327`, while those of PR #382 and PR #384 used `Refs`; and the first merge command of PR #381 passed a SHA whose last digits were made up, which GitHub refused. A PR in a GitHub stack (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`, Stacked PRs) merges from the command line only through the asynchronous merge API: `PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{n}/merge-async` with `merge_method` (`merge`), `merge_action` (`direct_merge`), `sha` (the head), `commit_title`, and `commit_message`, which answers 202 with a pending state; `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{n}/merge-async/{uuid}` then reads it as merged. GitHub's documentation says so: "If you merge via the API and want to use stacked pull requests, you'll need use the asynchronous merge API for stacks" (https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/how-tos/merge-and-close-pull-requests/merging-stacked-pull-requests). For PR #505 `gh pr merge` was refused ("must be merged using the asynchronous merge REST API") and so was `PUT /repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/505/merge` (403); the call above with `merge_method` `squash` merged it as 4758d74, with the title and the body of the form above (the session's calls, written down only in PR #505's retrospective comment). The same call with `merge_method` `merge` has not been run.
- `git branch --show-current` is part of the command that commits. A chain that creates a branch can fail before it does, and the commit then lands on `main`.
- A push is chained to the checks that `.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc` (Gates) asks before it, so that a failed check stops the push (`<checks>; if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) { git push }`).
- Before a commit, read `git status --short` and stage the files the change meant to touch; a file nobody meant to add is deleted, not committed. `git add -A` committed an empty `{}` (be8b64a) and an empty `devices-and-proofs.md` (d14c441) at the repository root, left by a mistyped shell redirection; each stayed on `main` until someone saw it. `git add -A` was used once more in the work of PR #377.
- Read `gh pr view <n> --json state` before pushing to a branch that has a PR, and before editing its body. A push to the branch of a merged PR recreates the deleted branch and reaches nothing; an edited body no longer matches what was merged. After PR #201 merged, a second commit was pushed and the body edited to describe it; the commit moved to the next PR and the body was restored. The read and the edit are one command that stops on the value: `test "$(gh pr view <n> --json state --jq .state)" = OPEN && gh pr edit <n> …`. A chain `gh pr view <n> --json state --jq .state && gh pr edit <n> …` goes on whatever the state is, because the first command succeeds: after PR #362 merged it printed `MERGED` and replaced the body, which was then put back. After the merge, answers to the owner's review and results that came later (the checks of a PR made ready while CI ran) go in a comment on the PR (PR #489). A PR that reads `OPEN` and is not a draft may be merged at any moment: it goes back to draft (`gh pr ready <n> --undo`) before more work is pushed to it (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`, step 6). PR #510 was merged before a lesson asked of it could be added, and the lesson went to a new Issue (#511).
- Run `gh` from inside the repository: the main checkout or the row's worktree, with body files given by absolute path. Outside a repository `gh pr comment` stops with "not a git repository". On 2026-11-27 such a call run from the scratchpad failed, and its drafts were removed with the folder before they were posted.
- Do not merge the branch of a PR that has not merged into another branch. Wait for that PR, or open the second PR on top of it (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`, Stacked PRs). After the first PR merges, the two histories have two merge bases; local git merges them cleanly (`git merge-tree`), while GitHub shows the second PR as conflicting and starts no `pull_request` run. In PR #367 the branch of PR #362 was merged in to set its roadmap row to `done`; once PR #362 had merged, PR #367 read `CONFLICTING`, and the owner chose to merge `main` into the branch.
- To push the earlier of two local commits and keep the later one local, push that commit by its SHA: `git push origin <sha>:refs/heads/<branch>`. When the commit to push comes after the one to keep, put them in order on a new branch first (`git branch <new> <base>`, then `git cherry-pick` each), check that the two branches hold the same tree (`git diff <old> <new>` prints nothing), and push from it. In V1c4a a fix to a measurement workflow was committed on top of the English pages, which could not be pushed before the Japanese pages; reordering and pushing the fix by its SHA sent only it (PR #249).
- If history was rewritten, put the SHA from before the rewrite on the first line of the reply.
- Never skip hooks (`--no-verify`) or signing unless the owner orders it.
- Never commit key material, a real license token, or a secret, test fixtures included (`.cursor/rules/security.mdc`). Read `git status --short` and the staged file list before each commit.
